#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:3
msgid "Quickstart"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:6
msgid "Installation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:8
msgid ""
"You can install the latest version of ``setuptools`` using :pypi:`pip`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:10
msgid "pip install --upgrade setuptools[core]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:12
msgid "Most of the time, however, you don't have to..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:14
msgid ""
"Instead, when creating new Python packages, it is recommended to use a "
"command line tool called :pypi:`build`. This tool will automatically "
"download ``setuptools`` and any other build-time dependencies that your "
"project might have. You just need to specify them in a ``pyproject.toml`` "
"file at the root of your package, as indicated in the :ref:`following "
"section <basic-use>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:22
msgid ""
"You can also :doc:`install build <build:how-to/install>` using :pypi:`pip`::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:24
msgid "pip install --upgrade build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:26
msgid "This will allow you to run the command: ``python -m build``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:29
msgid ""
"Please note that some operating systems might be equipped with the "
"``python3`` and ``pip3`` commands instead of ``python`` and ``pip`` (but "
"they should be equivalent). If you don't have ``pip`` or ``pip3`` available "
"in your system, please check out :doc:`pip installation docs <pip:"
"installation>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:36
msgid ""
"Every python package must provide a ``pyproject.toml`` and specify the "
"backend (build system) it wants to use. The distribution can then be "
"generated with whatever tool that provides a ``build sdist``-like "
"functionality."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:45
msgid "Basic Use"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:47
msgid ""
"When creating a Python package, you must provide a ``pyproject.toml`` file "
"containing a ``build-system`` section similar to the example below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:50
msgid ""
"[build-system]\n"
"requires = [\"setuptools\"]\n"
"build-backend = \"setuptools.build_meta\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:56
msgid ""
"This section declares what are your build system dependencies, and which "
"library will be used to actually do the packaging."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:61
msgid ""
"Package maintainers might be tempted to use ``setuptools[core]`` as the "
"requirement, given the guidance above. Avoid doing so, as the extra is "
"currently considered an internal implementation detail and is likely to go "
"away in the future and the Setuptools team will not support compatibility "
"for problems arising from packages published with this extra declared. "
"Vendored packages will satisfy the dependencies in the most common isolated "
"build scenarios."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:71
msgid ""
"Historically this documentation has unnecessarily listed ``wheel`` in the "
"``requires`` list, and many projects still do that. This is not recommended, "
"as the backend no longer requires the ``wheel`` package, and listing it "
"explicitly causes it to be unnecessarily required for source distribution "
"builds. You should only include ``wheel`` in ``requires`` if you need to "
"explicitly access it during build time (e.g. if your project needs a ``setup."
"py`` script that imports ``wheel``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:80
msgid ""
"In addition to specifying a build system, you also will need to add some "
"package information such as metadata, contents, dependencies, etc. This can "
"be done in the same ``pyproject.toml`` file, or in a separated one: ``setup."
"cfg`` or ``setup.py`` [#setup.py]_."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:85
msgid ""
"The following example demonstrates a minimum configuration (which assumes "
"the project depends on :pypi:`requests` and :pypi:`importlib-metadata` to be "
"able to run):"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:91
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"name = \"mypackage\"\n"
"version = \"0.0.1\"\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"requests\",\n"
"    'importlib-metadata; python_version<\"3.10\"',\n"
"]"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:101
msgid "See :doc:`/userguide/pyproject_config` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:105
msgid ""
"[metadata]\n"
"name = mypackage\n"
"version = 0.0.1\n"
"\n"
"[options]\n"
"install_requires =\n"
"    requests\n"
"    importlib-metadata; python_version<\"3.10\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:117
msgid "See :doc:`/userguide/declarative_config` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:121
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    name='mypackage',\n"
"    version='0.0.1',\n"
"    install_requires=[\n"
"        'requests',\n"
"        'importlib-metadata; python_version<\"3.10\"',\n"
"    ],\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:134
msgid "See :doc:`/references/keywords` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:136
msgid ""
"Finally, you will need to organize your Python code to make it ready for "
"distributing into something that looks like the following (optional files "
"marked with ``#``)::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:140
msgid ""
"mypackage\n"
"├── pyproject.toml  # and/or setup.cfg/setup.py (depending on the "
"configuration method)\n"
"|   # README.rst or README.md (a nice description of your package)\n"
"|   # LICENCE (properly chosen license information, e.g. MIT, BSD-3, GPL-3, "
"MPL-2, etc...)\n"
"└── mypackage\n"
"    ├── __init__.py\n"
"    └── ... (other Python files)"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:148
msgid ""
"With :ref:`build installed in your system <install-build>`, you can then "
"run::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:150
msgid "python -m build"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:152
msgid ""
"You now have your distribution ready (e.g. a ``tar.gz`` file and a ``.whl`` "
"file in the ``dist`` directory), which you can :doc:`upload <twine:index>` "
"to PyPI_!"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:155
msgid ""
"Of course, before you release your project to PyPI_, you'll want to add a "
"bit more information to help people find or learn about your project. And "
"maybe your project will have grown by then to include a few dependencies, "
"and perhaps some data files and scripts. In the next few sections, we will "
"walk through the additional but essential information you need to specify to "
"properly package your project."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:174
msgid "Info: Using ``setup.py``"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:177
msgid ""
"Setuptools offers first class support for ``setup.py`` files as a "
"configuration mechanism."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:180
msgid ""
"It is important to remember, however, that running this file as a script (e."
"g. ``python setup.py sdist``) is strongly **discouraged**, and that the "
"majority of the command line interfaces are (or will be) **deprecated** (e."
"g. ``python setup.py install``, ``python setup.py bdist_wininst``, ...)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:185
msgid ""
"We also recommend users to expose as much as possible configuration in a "
"more *declarative* way via the :doc:`pyproject.toml <pyproject_config>` or :"
"doc:`setup.cfg <declarative_config>`, and keep the ``setup.py`` minimal with "
"only the dynamic parts (or even omit it completely if applicable)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:190
msgid "See `Why you shouldn't invoke setup.py directly`_ for more background."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:196
msgid "Overview"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:199
msgid "Package discovery"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:200
msgid ""
"For projects that follow a simple directory structure, ``setuptools`` should "
"be able to automatically detect all :term:`packages <package>` and :term:"
"`namespaces <namespace package>`. However, complex projects might include "
"additional folders and supporting files that not necessarily should be "
"distributed (or that can confuse ``setuptools`` auto discovery algorithm)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:206
msgid ""
"Therefore, ``setuptools`` provides a convenient way to customize which "
"packages should be distributed and in which directory they should be found, "
"as shown in the example below:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:212
msgid ""
"# ...\n"
"[tool.setuptools.packages]\n"
"find = {}  # Scan the project directory with the default parameters\n"
"\n"
"# OR\n"
"[tool.setuptools.packages.find]\n"
"# All the following settings are optional:\n"
"where = [\"src\"]  # [\".\"] by default\n"
"include = [\"mypackage*\"]  # [\"*\"] by default\n"
"exclude = [\"mypackage.tests*\"]  # empty by default\n"
"namespaces = false  # true by default"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:228
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"packages = find: # OR `find_namespace:` if you want to use namespaces\n"
"\n"
"[options.packages.find]  # (always `find` even if `find_namespace:` was used "
"before)\n"
"# This section is optional as well as each of the following options:\n"
"where=src  # . by default\n"
"include=mypackage*  # * by default\n"
"exclude=mypackage.tests*  # empty by default"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:241
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup, find_packages  # or find_namespace_packages\n"
"\n"
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    packages=find_packages(\n"
"        # All keyword arguments below are optional:\n"
"        where='src',  # '.' by default\n"
"        include=['mypackage*'],  # ['*'] by default\n"
"        exclude=['mypackage.tests'],  # empty by default\n"
"    ),\n"
"    # ...\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:256
msgid ""
"When you pass the above information, alongside other necessary information, "
"``setuptools`` walks through the directory specified in ``where`` (defaults "
"to ``.``) and filters the packages it can find following the ``include`` "
"patterns (defaults to ``*``), then it removes those that match the "
"``exclude`` patterns (defaults to empty) and returns a list of Python "
"packages."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:261
msgid "For more details and advanced use, go to :ref:`package_discovery`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:264
msgid ""
"Starting with version 61.0.0, setuptools' automatic discovery capabilities "
"have been improved to detect popular project layouts (such as the :ref:`flat-"
"layout` and :ref:`src-layout`) without requiring any special configuration. "
"Check out our :ref:`reference docs <package_discovery>` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:272
msgid "Entry points and automatic script creation"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:273
msgid ""
"Setuptools supports automatic creation of scripts upon installation, that "
"run code within your package if you specify them as :doc:`entry points "
"<PyPUG:specifications/entry-points>`. An example of how this feature can be "
"used in ``pip``: it allows you to run commands like ``pip install`` instead "
"of having to type ``python -m pip install``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:280
msgid "The following configuration examples show how to accomplish this:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:285
msgid ""
"[project.scripts]\n"
"cli-name = \"mypkg.mymodule:some_func\""
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:292
msgid ""
"[options.entry_points]\n"
"console_scripts =\n"
"    cli-name = mypkg.mymodule:some_func"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:300
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    entry_points={\n"
"        'console_scripts': [\n"
"            'cli-name = mypkg.mymodule:some_func',\n"
"        ]\n"
"    }\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:311
msgid ""
"When this project is installed, a ``cli-name`` executable will be created. "
"``cli-name`` will invoke the function ``some_func`` in the ``mypkg/mymodule."
"py`` file when called by the user. Note that you can also use the ``entry-"
"points`` mechanism to advertise components between installed packages and "
"implement plugin systems. For detailed usage, go to :doc:`entry_point`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:320
msgid "Dependency management"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:321
msgid ""
"Packages built with ``setuptools`` can specify dependencies to be "
"automatically installed when the package itself is installed. The example "
"below shows how to configure this kind of dependencies:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:327
msgid ""
"[project]\n"
"# ...\n"
"dependencies = [\n"
"    \"docutils\",\n"
"    \"requests <= 0.4\",\n"
"]\n"
"# ..."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:339
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"install_requires =\n"
"    docutils\n"
"    requests <= 0.4"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:348
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    install_requires=[\"docutils\", \"requests <= 0.4\"],\n"
"    # ...\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:356
msgid ""
"Each dependency is represented by a string that can optionally contain "
"version requirements (e.g. one of the operators <, >, <=, >=, == or !=, "
"followed by a version identifier), and/or conditional environment markers, e."
"g. ``sys_platform == \"win32\"`` (see :doc:`PyPUG:specifications/version-"
"specifiers` for more information)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:361
msgid ""
"When your project is installed, all of the dependencies not already "
"installed will be located (via PyPI), downloaded, built (if necessary), and "
"installed. This, of course, is a simplified scenario. You can also specify "
"groups of extra dependencies that are not strictly required by your package "
"to work, but that will provide additional functionalities. For more advanced "
"use, see :doc:`dependency_management`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:372
msgid "Including Data Files"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:373
msgid ""
"Setuptools offers three ways to specify data files to be included in your "
"packages. For the simplest use, you can simply use the "
"``include_package_data`` keyword:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:378
msgid ""
"[tool.setuptools]\n"
"include-package-data = true\n"
"# This is already the default behaviour if you are using\n"
"# pyproject.toml to configure your build.\n"
"# You can deactivate that with `include-package-data = false`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:388
msgid ""
"[options]\n"
"include_package_data = True"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:395
msgid ""
"setup(\n"
"    # ...\n"
"    include_package_data=True,\n"
"    # ...\n"
")"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:403
msgid ""
"This tells setuptools to install any data files it finds in your packages. "
"The data files must be specified via the :ref:`MANIFEST.in <Using MANIFEST."
"in>` file or automatically added by a :ref:`Revision Control System plugin "
"<Adding Support for Revision Control Systems>`. For more details, see :doc:"
"`datafiles`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:411
msgid "Development mode"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:413
msgid ""
"``setuptools`` allows you to install a package without copying any files to "
"your interpreter directory (e.g. the ``site-packages`` directory). This "
"allows you to modify your source code and have the changes take effect "
"without you having to rebuild and reinstall. Here's how to do it::"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:419
msgid "pip install --editable ."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:421
msgid "See :doc:`development_mode` for more information."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:425
msgid ""
"Prior to :ref:`pip v21.1 <pip:v21-1>`, a ``setup.py`` script was required to "
"be compatible with development mode. With late versions of pip, projects "
"without ``setup.py`` may be installed in this mode."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:429
msgid ""
"If you have a version of ``pip`` older than v21.1 or is using a different "
"packaging-related tool that does not support :pep:`660`, you might need to "
"keep a ``setup.py`` file in your repository if you want to use editable "
"installs."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:434
msgid "A simple script will suffice, for example:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:436
msgid ""
"from setuptools import setup\n"
"\n"
"setup()"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:442
msgid ""
"You can still keep all the configuration in :doc:`pyproject.toml </userguide/"
"pyproject_config>` and/or :doc:`setup.cfg </userguide/declarative_config>`"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:448
msgid ""
"When building from source code (for example, by ``python -m build`` or ``pip "
"install -e .``) some directories hosting build artefacts and cache files may "
"be created, such as ``build``, ``dist``, ``*.egg-info`` [#cache]_. You can "
"configure your version control system to ignore them (see `GitHub's ."
"gitignore template <https://github.com/github/gitignore/blob/main/Python."
"gitignore>`_ for an example)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:459
msgid "Uploading your package to PyPI"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:460
msgid ""
"After generating the distribution files, the next step would be to upload "
"your distribution so others can use it. This functionality is provided by :"
"pypi:`twine` and is documented in the :doc:`Python packaging tutorial <PyPUG:"
"tutorials/packaging-projects>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:467
msgid "Transitioning from ``setup.py`` to declarative config"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:468
msgid ""
"To avoid executing arbitrary scripts and boilerplate code, we are "
"transitioning from defining all your package information by running "
"``setup()`` to doing this declaratively - by using ``pyproject.toml`` (or "
"older ``setup.cfg``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:472
msgid ""
"To ease the challenges of transitioning, we provide a quick :doc:`guide </"
"userguide/pyproject_config>` to understanding how ``pyproject.toml`` is "
"parsed by ``setuptools``. (Alternatively, here is the :doc:`guide </"
"userguide/declarative_config>` for ``setup.cfg``)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:479
msgid ""
"The approach ``setuptools`` would like to take is to eventually use a single "
"declarative format (``pyproject.toml``) instead of maintaining 2 "
"(``pyproject.toml`` / ``setup.cfg``). Yet, chances are, ``setup.cfg`` will "
"continue to be maintained for a long time."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:487
msgid "Resources on Python packaging"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:488
msgid ""
"Packaging in Python can be hard and is constantly evolving. `Python "
"Packaging User Guide <https://packaging.python.org>`_ has tutorials and up-"
"to-date references that can help you when it is time to distribute your work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:497
msgid "Notes"
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:499
msgid ""
"New projects are advised to avoid ``setup.py`` configurations (beyond the "
"minimal stub) when custom scripting during the build is not necessary. "
"Examples are kept in this document to help people interested in maintaining "
"or contributing to existing packages that use ``setup.py``. Note that you "
"can still keep most of configuration declarative in :doc:`setup.cfg "
"<declarative_config>` or :doc:`pyproject.toml <pyproject_config>` and use "
"``setup.py`` only for the parts not supported in those files (e.g. C "
"extensions). See :ref:`note <setuppy_discouraged>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/quickstart.rst:510
msgid ""
"If you feel that caching is causing problems to your build, specially after "
"changes in the configuration files, consider removing ``build``, ``dist``, "
"``*.egg-info`` before rebuilding or installing your project."
msgstr ""
